patch: unaligned-simon-c-helpers
why: Static helper functions for the memcpy-based word accesses of the other unaligned-* patches of this file (no new symbols).
file: upstream/ref/Simon.c
count: 1
find: void SimonKeySetup64(
replace: static __inline unsigned int jb_ld32(const void *p, size_t i) { unsigned int v; { size_t j; for (j = 0; j < 4; j++) ((unsigned char *)&v)[j] = ((const unsigned char *)p)[4 * i + j]; } return v; } static __inline uint64_t jb_ld64(const void *p, size_t i) { uint64_t v; { size_t j; for (j = 0; j < 8; j++) ((unsigned char *)&v)[j] = ((const unsigned char *)p)[8 * i + j]; } return v; } static __inline uint64_t jb_ld48(const void *p, size_t i) { uint64_t v; v = 0; { size_t j; for (j = 0; j < 6; j++) ((unsigned char *)&v)[j] = ((const unsigned char *)p)[8 * i + j]; } return v; } void SimonKeySetup64(
patch: unaligned-encrypt_simon96-c-line-36
why: The reference code reads and writes block words through casts of byte pointers (for example ((uint64_t *)(c + msglen))[0]), which
why: are misaligned typed accesses (undefined behaviour, UBSan "misaligned address" in CI run 45610244076). This 96-bit variant masks
why: its words to 48 bits, so the patch moves exactly the 6 bytes the algorithm uses (memcpy into a zeroed or from a 64-bit local) instead of 8:
why: the 48 bits are the same value as the old masked 8-byte access (the 2 extra bytes were masked off or were overwritten by the next block), and the
why: access no longer runs 2 bytes past the block. Output is bit-identical.
file: upstream/ref/encrypt_simon96.c
count: 1
find: ((uint64_t *)(c + msglen))[0] = (stateS[0] ^ stateS[1] ^ stateR[0]) & 0xffffffffffff;
replace: jb_st48((c + msglen), 0, (stateS[0] ^ stateS[1] ^ stateR[0]) & 0xffffffffffff);
